--TEST--
A reference to a synthesized `constructor` resolves, and a written call to one is refused (`rule:attributes/structural-retrieval`, `rule:classes/definite-property-initialization`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
type Route = {path: string};

#[Route(path: "/a")]
class Plain {}

// Accepted first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here rather
// than as a missing refusal. `rule:classes/definite-property-initialization` gives every class a constructor,
// definitely, so `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval`'s class target names it whether or not the body
// writes one.
?Route $route = Core\Attributes::get<Route>(Plain::constructor(...));
echo $route != null ? "some" : "none", "\n";

// The exemption is that one spelling and no wider: a written call resolves to
// no signature at all, so it would reach `nvs-ir` with no target recorded.
Plain::constructor();

class Sub extends Plain {
    public function constructor() {
        // The ported-PHP spelling, and the same refusal: `Plain` writes no
        // constructor for `parent::` to name.
        parent::constructor();
    }
}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0309]: `Plain` has no method named `constructor`
   --> case.nvs:16:1
   |
16 | Plain::constructor();
   | ^^^^^ referenced here

error[E0309]: `Plain` has no method named `constructor`
   --> case.nvs:22:9
   |
22 |         parent::constructor();
   |         ^^^^^^ referenced here

error: aborting due to 2 errors
